WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:09.688
What is Ohm's law? When electricity passes through a wire, something pushes it along. That push is called voltage, and we measure it in volts.

00:00:09.808 --> 00:00:13.460
The flow is called current; this meter reads it in amperes.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.798
Each wire holds the flow back; that is resistance, in ohms.

00:00:17.925 --> 00:00:22.965
Ohm's law says the current goes up with the push, as long as the temperature stays the same.

00:00:23.103 --> 00:00:30.212
So voltage equals current times resistance: V equals I R. Then I is V over R, and R is V over I.

00:00:30.378 --> 00:00:33.357
Two volts over ten ohms gives point two amperes.

00:00:33.481 --> 00:00:37.389
Double the push to four volts, and the current doubles to point four.

00:00:37.832 --> 00:00:43.317
Keep two volts, double the resistance to twenty ohms, and the current halves to point one.

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:48.585
Voltage against current makes a straight line through zero. Its slope is the resistance.

00:00:48.705 --> 00:00:52.591
But a hot bulb's line bends, because its filament resists more.

00:00:52.715 --> 00:00:54.548
Twice the push, twice the flow.

00:00:54.996 --> 00:00:56.915
Try more cases in the free lesson.

00:00:57.049 --> 00:00:58.658
Professor Diki Simplified.
